{
  "id": "Compound::DB01601 - Gene::5243",
  "verbalization": "Lopinavir is associated with Abcb1 through a series of molecular interactions that involve the P-glycoprotein family and Cytochrome P450 3a enzymes. The evidence suggests that Lopinavir, as a member of the Cytochrome P450 3a Inhibitors class, binds to Ivacaftor or Lomitapide, which in turn interact with Abcb1. This pathway highlights a potential mechanism by which Lopinavir may influence the activity of the ABCB1 transporter, possibly by modulating its expression or function. The shared lowest common ancestor of Lopinavir and these compounds—Carboxamide—suggests a common biochemical pathway or regulatory mechanism that links these drugs to Abcb1. The integration of these pathways implies that Lopinavir may exert its effects on Abcb1 through a mechanism involving the P-glycoprotein family, potentially by inhibiting the transporter or altering its activity. This relationship underscores the role of ABCB1 in drug resistance and transport, and suggests that Lopinavir may contribute to this process through its interaction with these inhibitors.",
